Scores rather than raw distribution parameters, because a score is what
collaborators actually consume and it is dimensionless. An absolute tolerance
on \code{mu} is meaningless without knowing your response's scale -- multiply your
outcome by 10,000 and \code{mu}'s error multiplies by 10,000 too -- and a single
tolerance cannot suit \code{mu}, \code{sigma} and \code{nu} at once, since they live on
different scales within one model. Score error is invariant to all of that,
and it is the only comparison that still means something when the two models
have different families and so no common parameters at all.

z-scores rather than the centiles they come from, because centile differences
are compressed in the tails. The same underlying discrepancy that shifts a
score by a given amount produces a large centile difference near the median
and a vanishing one at the extremes, so a centile-based tolerance silently
under-weights exactly the tail errors that matter most for screening. In
z units the error is roughly flat across the distribution -- for a location
shift it is simply \verb{d mu / sigma} -- so one threshold means the same thing
everywhere. The transform is \code{qnorm(centile)}, matching
\code{\link[=score_centiles]{score_centiles()}} with \code{standardize = TRUE}.

For each requested centile the \code{reference} model's own quantile function gives
the response value at that centile; both models are then asked what score they
assign to it. Taking \code{reference}'s quantiles (rather than the nominal centile)
as the comparison point cancels the round-trip error of \verb{q<family>()} /
\verb{p<family>()}, so what remains is the disagreement between the models. This is
the one asymmetry between the two arguments: \code{reference} decides \emph{where} in the
distribution the comparison happens.
}
\section{Choosing each model's prediction path}{

Passing a model's fitting data routes it through \code{gamlss::predictAll()} with
the data in scope; leaving the argument \code{NULL} reconstructs its parameters
data-free. Which you want depends on the question:
\itemize{
\item verifying a sanitized model, leave both \code{NULL} -- the check then isolates
what sanitizing changed, and nothing else;
\item validating the whole chain a collaborator depends on (data-based ->
data-free -> sanitized), pass \code{reference_data} and leave \code{comparison_data}
\code{NULL}. This is the stronger check to run before sharing;
\item a model with a term that cannot be reconstructed data-free (see
\code{\link[=sanitize_gamlss]{sanitize_gamlss()}}) must be given its data, or prediction errors rather
than guessing.
}

Note that a data-based reference is not an infallible one. \code{predictAll()}
rebuilds the design over the fitting data and \code{newdata} together and re-solves
for the coefficients in that basis, which is exact for most models but only a
projection when a term's basis moves with the data.
}

\examples{
iris_model <- gamlss(Sepal.Width ~ pb(Sepal.Length) + Species,
                     data = iris, family = NO, trace = FALSE)
clean <- sanitize_gamlss(iris_model)

# Evaluate OFF the rebuild grid. sim_grid() returns 500 points and grid_n
# defaults to 500, so sim_grid() output aliases exactly onto the rebuilt
# spline's nodes -- where it is exact by construction -- and the check would
# report 0. Any count other than grid_n avoids this; compare_zscores()
# warns if you hit it.
nd <- data.frame(
  Sepal.Length = seq(min(iris$Sepal.Length), max(iris$Sepal.Length),
                     length.out = 997),
  Species      = factor("setosa", levels = levels(iris$Species)))

# what sanitizing alone changed
compare_zscores(iris_model, clean, nd)

# the whole chain, against the data-based path
compare_zscores(iris_model, clean, nd, reference_data = iris)

# no sanitizing: what gamlss's own prediction machinery contributes
compare_zscores(iris_model, iris_model, nd, reference_data = iris)

# two different models of the same outcome
simpler <- gamlss(Sepal.Width ~ Sepal.Length + Species,
                  data = iris, family = NO, trace = FALSE)
compare_zscores(iris_model, simpler, nd, tol = 0.1)

}
